WHATNOW

What tam-last is doing now and what comes next. Read this first; revise
it at the end of every session (work/README-work).

Updated: 2026-11-17, with W4


STATE

  Version 0.0.2 on branch dev, pushed. Fred accepted its text on DP-2
  (scale 1.25) beside foot (bug B1, verified); FreeType stays out.

  What works: our own Wayland client, TrueType rasterizer with vertical
  grid fitting, fractional scaling, US keymap, legacy xterm key
  encoding, a shell on a pty, cursor movement, erasing, insert and
  delete, colors and attributes, wide characters. readelf -d lists only
  libc and libm. make test and make sanitize pass.

  Recent: W1 built 0.0.1; W2 published to GitHub; W3 wrote the docs and
  fixed B1 (0.0.2); W4 set up this file.


NEXT

  1. 0.0.3, full-screen programs (plans/P1-foundation.md has the list
     and the acceptance checks). Set VERSION to 0.0.3 in the first code
     commit; make test checks tam-last --version.

  2. Scroll regions (DECSTBM) touch every scroll in src/term.c:
     linefeed, ESC M, IL and DL (CSI L, M) and SU and SD (CSI S, T) call
     scroll_up and scroll_down with the whole screen as the region.
     Give those two a bottom margin as well as a top, and route every
     caller through the region. Add origin mode and IRM with it.

  3. The alternate screen (47, 1047, 1049) needs a second grid and its
     own saved cursor. tl_term_resize must handle both grids.

  4. DEC special graphics (ESC ( 0), combining characters stored with
     their base cell, and cursor styles (DECSCUSR, no blinking unless
     asked).

  5. Damage tracking: every frame redraws everything today (render.c,
     draw() in main.c). Track dirty rows in tl_term, draw and damage
     only those, and keep the full redraw for resizes and scale
     changes. Damage is in buffer pixels (damage_buffer). The two
     buffers alternate, so keep damage per buffer.

  Done when: the P1 acceptance checks pass, and Fred has run vim, htop,
  less and yazi in it.


